7
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:50,920
We can find low cost with single phase input or high accuracy with polyphase approach.

8
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:55,160
The main one is the induction motor with three phase.

9
00:00:55,160 --> 00:01:01,200
Right, you can see the typical structure of an induction motor.

10
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,800
The most common type is the squirrel cage.

11
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:14,900
You can see the stator incorporated with three phases armature windings, you can see the rotor

12
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:21,900
incorporated with cage bars that are short-circuited by two hand rings.

13
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:30,380
You can find two bearings to the mechanical support of the rotor and a machine frame with two

14
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:32,900
hand belts towards the machine.

15
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:40,900
The windings in the stator are applied at three phase electrical power that establish a rotating

16
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:45,900
magnetic field in the air gap between the stator and the rotor.

17
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:55,900
The stator field can be seen as a series of north-south poles revolving around the circumferences

18
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:56,900
of the stator.

19
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:05,900
The rotating speed of the stator magnetic field is linked to the supply frequency f.

20
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:12,320
In an induction motor, the angular speed of the rotor is lower than the synchronous speed

21
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:18,900
of the magnetic field generated by the stator windings, hence called asynchronous.

22
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:25,900
The rotating magnetic field induces currents in the rotor by magnetic induction.

23
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:35,900
These currents, in turn, generate a rotor magnetic field which opposes flux variations.

24
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:43,900
It interacts with the stator field generating a torque on the rotor windings which causes the

25
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:45,900
rotor to rotate.

26
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:52,900
So, just to recap, we have a current in the stator windings that creates a stator magnetic

27
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:53,900
field.

28
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:00,900
This stator magnetic field induces a current in the rotor by magnetic induction.

29
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:04,900
The rotor current creates a rotor magnetic field.

30
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:13,900
The interaction between this magnetic field generates a torque that causes the rotor to rotate.

31
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:20,900
The synchronous speed is linked to the pole paves of the motor and the frequency.

32
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:27,900
Because the relative motion between the rotating field and the rotator is the key of torque production,

33
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:36,900
the slip s is defined as you can see in this slide, where omega s represents the synchronous

34
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:41,900
speed and omega r is the rotor speed.

35
